1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,212
Studio Design is a skill:

2
00:00:02,212 --> 00:00:05,196
instructions, recipes, editable starters, and local helpers.

3
00:00:05,196 --> 00:00:06,902
Invoke it inside Codex.

4
00:00:06,902 --> 00:00:09,033
The website showcases working examples;

5
00:00:09,033 --> 00:00:12,018
it is not the skill’s product interface.

6
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:14,794
Let your agent handle setup.

7
00:00:14,794 --> 00:00:17,614
Copy the installation prompt from the README.

8
00:00:17,614 --> 00:00:22,448
Your agent reviews the repository, chooses a skills folder for your harness,

9
00:00:22,448 --> 00:00:25,671
checks the dry run, and prepares local tools.

10
00:00:25,671 --> 00:00:28,491
Reload if needed, then activate Studio Design.

11
00:00:29,247 --> 00:00:32,705
Give the audience, primary task, reference, and output.

12
00:00:32,705 --> 00:00:33,570
For example:

13
00:00:33,570 --> 00:00:39,190
a reading-room prototype, calm and dense directions, the Harbor style, and editable HTML.

14
00:00:39,190 --> 00:00:42,649
Codex reads project instructions and the relevant recipes.

15
00:00:43,413 --> 00:00:49,885
Thirteen core modes cover interfaces, mobile, wireframes, documents, resumes, fliers, HTML presentations,

16
00:00:49,885 --> 00:00:56,357
animation, three D objects, research, email, diagrams, and color and type systems.

17
00:00:56,357 --> 00:00:59,593
Read only the recipes you need.

18
00:01:00,347 --> 00:01:03,626
Here, choose two interests to enter the reading room.

19
00:01:03,626 --> 00:01:06,905
Save an article, check validation, and confirm the result.

20
00:01:06,905 --> 00:01:08,727
Test recovery and keyboard behavior.

21
00:01:08,727 --> 00:01:12,735
This real prototype uses local sample data, with no service connection.

22
00:01:13,513 --> 00:01:15,383
A canvas compares working artboards.

23
00:01:15,383 --> 00:01:19,122
Inspect hierarchy and density, focus an option, and try Save.

24
00:01:19,122 --> 00:01:22,487
Connected local previews save supported edits to selected files;

25
00:01:22,487 --> 00:01:25,105
public demos keep edits in the browser.

26
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:28,151
Composition Stage keeps a persistent tree.

27
00:01:28,151 --> 00:01:31,936
Shots, animations, and captions follow authored time and named cues.

28
00:01:31,936 --> 00:01:35,721
Stretch a section or change speed without cutting its choreography.

29
00:01:35,721 --> 00:01:37,993
This tutorial uses that same engine.

30
00:01:38,747 --> 00:01:40,174
Serve over loopback.

31
00:01:40,174 --> 00:01:44,456
Exercise the flow, inspect screenshots, and check the console.

32
00:01:44,456 --> 00:01:49,213
Export HTML, PNG, PDF, PowerPoint, or MP4 with local helpers.

33
00:01:49,213 --> 00:01:51,116
Keep the source folder:

34
00:01:51,116 --> 00:01:54,922
the HTML and code remain editable after export.

35
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:59,834
PowerPoint supports editable slides with native builds and local media.

36
00:01:59,834 --> 00:02:02,326
Complex CSS can need image layers.

37
00:02:02,326 --> 00:02:06,480
Check fonts, animation, and media playback in the receiving application.

38
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:10,634
External generation and transfers need configured providers or authorized connectors.

39
00:02:10,634 --> 00:02:15,619
Give a concrete brief, verify the result, and keep the editable artifact.
